// 19_strarity.voi — a surplus argument to a str native is refused, loudly.
//
// `trim_left(s)` strips whitespace and takes the subject alone; the cutset
// form is `strip(s, cutset)` (§13.2). `str.trim_left("..x", ".")` used to
// return "..x" — a trim that silently did nothing — because a native carries
// no static signature, so nothing counted its arguments and the surplus one
// was never read.
//
// The surplus argument is now spelled as a METHOD, and that is the point of
// this file rather than an accident of style. chk/native.voi refuses the
// QUALIFIED spelling at compile time (tests/negative/nativearity.voi records
// that), but it cannot judge a method call: the receiver is an ordinary
// expression and its type is what would decide, which is expression typing
// and does not exist yet. So the method spelling is the one that still
// reaches runtime/src/str.c's str_check_arity, and it is what keeps the
// RUNTIME guard tested now that the compile-time one covers the rest.
//
// The abort is the point of the test, so it is the LAST thing main does.
package main
use "std/str"

func main() {
    // Correct calls, including the optional-argument forms, still work.
    say str.trim_left("  x")
    say str.strip("..x", ".")
    say str.substr("hello", 2, 3)
    say str.substr("hello", 2)

    // ...and the surplus one aborts, at run time, through the one spelling
    // the checker cannot judge.
    say "..x".trim_left(".")
    say "NOT REACHED"
}
